+# Quickstart: validate Codex SessionEnd Budget Clamp
+
+**Phase 1 output** of `__SPECKIT_COMMAND_PLAN__`.
+
+Runnable validation that proves the feature works end-to-end from this checkout, using the smallest adequate observers: the existing in-process hook test module plus targeted byte/read checks. No network, Docker, credentials, or browser. **Python 3 on PATH** (this machine has CPython 3.11.9). All commands assume the repository root is the working directory. Report passed / failed / unobserved as separate lines; do not fold them into one verdict.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- Python 3 on PATH.
+- Windows PowerShell (primary) or a POSIX shell.
+- This checkout (the single source of truth for the run).
+
+Set these once (PowerShell):
+
+```powershell
+cd <repo-root>
+$hookMain      = "plugins\graphhelm\hooks\session_hook.py"
+$hookCompanion = "plugins\graphhelm-codex-hooks\hooks\session_hook.py"
+$cxMain        = "plugins\graphhelm\hooks\codex-hooks.json"
+$cxCompanion   = "plugins\graphhelm-codex-hooks\hooks\codex-hooks.json"
+$claude        = "plugins\graphhelm\hooks\hooks.json"
+```
+
+## S1. Byte-identity of the paired files (FR-007 · US3 · SC-004)
+
+Both comparisons must print `True` (each pair byte-identical after editing).
+
+```powershell
+(Get-FileHash $hookMain).Hash -eq (Get-FileHash $hookCompanion).Hash
+(Get-FileHash $cxMain).Hash   -eq (Get-FileHash $cxCompanion).Hash
+```
+
+A `False` means the corresponding pair drifted and the single-source invariant is broken.
+
+## S2. Registered budgets match the host clamps (FR-001/004/005 · US1-A3 · SC-001)
+
+Read the declared `timeout` values and check the exact matrix from [`contracts/hook-budget-matrix.md`](contracts/hook-budget-matrix.md):
+
+```powershell
+function Get-TO([string]$f,[string]$evt){ (Get-Content $f -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json).hooks.$evt[0].hooks[0].timeout }
+"codex SessionEnd main       -> " (Get-TO $cxMain "SessionEnd")        # expect 3
+"codex SessionEnd companion  -> " (Get-TO $cxCompanion "SessionEnd")   # expect 3
+"codex SessionStart main     -> " (Get-TO $cxMain "SessionStart")      # expect 5
+"codex SessionStart companion-> " (Get-TO $cxCompanion "SessionStart") # expect 5
+"claude SessionStart         -> " (Get-TO $claude "SessionStart")      # expect 5
+"claude SessionEnd           -> " (Get-TO $claude "SessionEnd")        # expect 5
+```
+
+Assert: both codex SessionEnd == 3 (== the Codex clamp); every other line above == 5. Then assert the end wait fits inside Codex's budget, stays above the original 1 s limit, and leaves margin over the test's 1.25 s ack:
+
+```powershell
+# The only inline (non-default) wait literal is the end POST; the start GET uses the default timeout=1.0 parameter.
+$w = (Select-String -Path $hookMain -Pattern 'timeout=([0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]+)?)\)' | Select-Object -First 1 | ForEach-Object { $_.Matches[0].Groups[1].Value })
+[double]$w          # expect 2.0
+1.0 -lt [double]$w -and [double]$w -lt 3.0   # FR-002 + FR-003 -> expect True
+[double]$w -gt 1.25                               # keeps the behavioral slow-ack test green -> expect True
+```
+
+## S3. Hook test suite is green (FR-008 · SC-004) — the behavioral observer
+
+Run the in-process hook test module — scoped to this single file, **not** the whole workspace build or full Rust suite:
+
+```powershell
+python plugins\graphhelm\hooks\test_session_hook.py
+```
+
+Expect `Ran NN tests ... OK` with **zero** failures/errors. This one observer proves both the structural invariant (byte-identity/wiring test) and the delivery behaviors the budgets constrain: a slow ack within the wait is confirmed; refusal/timeout leaves the end unobserved and retryable with the same body and idempotency identity; a confirmed repeat end skips the send; and a killed hook releases its lock and retries the same signal.
+
+Acceptance scenario → committed observer (test name):
+
+| Scenario | Observer(s) in `test_session_hook.py` |
+|----------|----------------------------------------|
+| US1-A1: ack within shortened wait confirmed; no re-send | `test_end_accepts_slow_matching_ack_without_extra_posts`, `test_end_posts_an_attributed_idempotent_observation_without_success_claim` |
+| US1-A2: silence beyond wait → unobserved, retryable, same identity | `test_failed_delivery_is_retryable_with_the_same_signal_bytes`, `test_runtime_refusal_is_unobserved_not_recorded`, `test_mismatched_ack_is_unobserved_and_remains_retryable`, `test_killed_end_hook_releases_lock_and_retries_same_signal` |
+| US1-A3: both Codex files declare the 3 s budget | S2 above + `test_codex_compatibility_package_keeps_one_hook_source` |
+| US2: guide matches registered values | S4 below |
+| US3: single source of truth intact | S1 above + S3 (full module green) |
+
+## S4. Documented budgets contradict zero registrations (US2 · SC-003 · FR-006)
+
+Open `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`, the "Session hooks" budget paragraph. Verify by reading it:
+
+- The end-acknowledgement wait is stated as **two seconds of socket inactivity**, and that this fits inside Codex's SessionEnd budget.
+- Codex SessionEnd is stated as **declared and clamped at three seconds**.
+- Claude Code keeps **five seconds** for both its hooks.
+- The two honesty sentences are present verbatim: *"The socket limit alone is not a wall-time deadline."* and *"A timeout remains unobserved; it never becomes task success."*
+- The old incorrect claim that *both hosts* bound SessionEnd to five seconds no longer appears:
+
+```powershell
+# No sentence may still say BOTH hosts bound SessionEnd to five seconds.
+(Select-String -Path "plugins\graphhelm\README.md" -Pattern 'Both hosts bound' | Measure-Object).Count   # expect 0
+```
+
+## S5. (Optional, strongest) Probe the shortened wait near 1.5 s (SC-002)
+
+If a standalone ~1.5 s confirmation is wanted beyond the module's built-in 1.25 s case, reuse the module's own harness semantics: a stand-in Runtime that releases the ack after ~1.5 s must see the hook mark delivery (inside the 2.0 s wait), while a silent stand-in past 2.0 s must leave the end unobserved and retryable. The committed module already exercises exactly these two outcomes (confirmed at 1.25 s; unobserved via refusal/timeout), so **S3 is the committed observer**; S5 is an extra confidence probe only if a reviewer asks for a different instant.
+
+## Out of scope for validation
+
+Do **not** run the workspace-wide cargo build or the full Rust test suite for this change: the touched surface is the Python plugin package, and its own in-process module is the adequate observer. When this work later merges, the repository's normal gate/review/merge procedure governs (per `AGENTS.md`); that procedure is applied at merge time, not during this planning run.
+
+## Done when
+
+S1 prints `True` twice, S2 prints the exact expected matrix (both codex SessionEnd = 3, all others = 5, end wait ≈ 2.0 with `1.0 < w < 3.0` and `w > 1.25`), S3 prints `OK` with zero failures, S4 passes all five checks (including the `Both hosts bound` count of 0), and the two Codex registration pairs plus the two script pairs remain byte-identical. Any unmet condition is reported on its own line as passed / failed / unobserved.
