+# Research: Codex SessionEnd Budget Clamp
+
+**Phase 0 output** of `__SPECKIT_COMMAND_PLAN__`. Resolves every provisional item in the plan's Technical Context. Each finding is written as **Decision / Rationale / Alternatives considered**. All values were read directly from the checkout at HEAD `e6ef2719`; none are assumed.
+
+## R0. Constitution substitution (recorded)
+
+**Decision**: This checkout has no `.specify/memory/constitution.md`. Per this run's stage setup, the repository-root **`AGENTS.md`** is used as the governance reference for the Constitution Check.
+
+**Rationale**: The stage setup explicitly instructs this substitution, and `AGENTS.md` carries the repository's Journey-Proven Development, security, documentation-language, and delivery rules.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Treat the missing constitution as a gate failure (ERROR). Rejected — the substitution was mandated by the run, and it is recorded in both `plan.md` and the spec's Assumptions.
+
+## R1. The exact defect: three different numbers, one race
+
+**Measured current values (HEAD `e6ef2719`):**
+
+| Concept | Value | Location |
+|---------|-------|----------|
+| Codex SessionEnd **registered** budget | `"timeout": 5` | both `codex-hooks.json`, line 23 |
+| Codex SessionEnd **host-enforced clamp** | 3 s | installed Codex (external; not in these files) |
+| Shared end **socket-inactivity wait** | `timeout=3.0` | both `session_hook.py`, line 351 |
+| SessionStart **socket-inactivity wait** | default `timeout=1.0` | `session_hook.py`, line 87 (signature default) |
+
+**Rationale for the race**: the host's three-second wall clock starts when it launches the hook process and includes Python interpreter start-up + imports + loopback/TCP connect + the socket wait. With a 3.0 s socket wait, the process can still be waiting well past three seconds of wall time, so Codex kills it mid-wait. A durable acknowledgement that arrives at, say, 2.5–3.0 s is lost in flight. Separately, the registration over-declares (5 > 3), so operators believe there is more budget than the host honors.
+
+**Decision**: make all three consistent — declare 3 (matches the clamp) and wait strictly less than 3 (fits inside the clamp).
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- Only fix the declarations and keep the 3.0 s wait — rejected; the race is wall-time vs socket-inactivity, and 3.0 still lets the process outlive the 3 s clamp once start-up is added.
+- Declare a value below the clamp and wait longer — impossible; the declared value must not exceed the host clamp for it to be meaningful.
+
+## R2. The concrete end-acknowledgement wait value W
+
+**Decision**: **W = 2.0 seconds** of socket inactivity for the end POST, in both `session_hook.py` copies.
+
+**Constraints that bound W:**
+- FR-002: `W < 3.0` (strictly inside the Codex budget/clamp).
+- FR-003: `W > 1.0` (retain the earlier extension that accepts a durable ack slower than the original 1 s limit).
+- Test compatibility (so `test_end_accepts_slow_matching_ack_without_extra_posts` stays green unmodified — the spec's Assumptions say tests are "expected to pass unmodified"): that test holds the ack and releases it after a **fixed 1.25 s**, then asserts `delivered == True`. For the socket wait to still be open when the bytes arrive at 1.25 s plus flush/network slack, W must be comfortably > 1.25 s.
+- Wall-time headroom (the actual bug being fixed): `3.0 − W` must leave room for process start-up + connect. CPython cold start on Windows is typically ~0.1–0.3 s but can spike; loopback/TCP connect is sub-millisecond.
+
+**Candidate evaluation:**
+
+| Candidate | FR-002 (<3.0) | FR-003 (>1.0) | Test margin (W−1.25) | Wall headroom (3.0−W) | Verdict |
+|-----------|:-------------:|:-------------:|:--------------------:|:---------------------:|---------|
+| 1.5 | ✓ | ✓ | 0.25 s (tight vs CI jitter) | 1.5 s | viable but thin on the test side |
+| **2.0** | ✓ | ✓ | 0.75 s | 1.0 s | **chosen — balanced** |
+| 2.5 | ✓ | ✓ | 1.25 s | 0.5 s (thin for Windows cold start) | viable but thin on the clamp side |
+
+**Rationale**: 2.0 s balances the two margins — ≥0.75 s of socket margin over the fixed 1.25 s test ack (so the behavioral test cannot flake) and a full 1.0 s of headroom under the host clamp (so interpreter start-up plus connect fit inside the clamp). It is clean and memorable, and it keeps the existing suite green without editing any test literal.
+
+**Alternatives considered and rejected:**
+- **2.5 s** — maximizes accepted-slow-acks and test margin, but leaves only 0.5 s for process start-up under the 3 s wall clock; on a cold/slow Windows CPython start that is exactly the headroom the bug consumed, so it re-risks the mid-wait kill.
+- **1.5 s** — more clamp headroom, but only 0.25 s of socket margin over the test's 1.25 s release; a loaded CI host pushing bytes across that gap could flip `delivered` and turn a green behavioral test red — reintroducing the timing brittleness this change removes.
+- **2.75 s** — even thinner wall headroom (0.25 s); rejected for the same reason as 2.5 s, worse.
+- **Keep 3.0 s, fix declarations only** — rejected (see R1): the race is wall-time vs socket-inactivity.
+
+## R3. Exact edit surface (measured)
+
+Files and the precise edit in each (all literals confirmed by reading the files at HEAD):
+
+1. `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/codex-hooks.json` — SessionEnd `"timeout": 5` → `"timeout": 3` (line 23). Leave SessionStart `"timeout": 5` (line 11) and the `statusMessage`/`additionalContextLimit` fields untouched.
+2. `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/codex-hooks.json` — identical edit (keeps the pair byte-identical, FR-007).
+3. `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py` — end POST `timeout=3.0` → `timeout=2.0` (line 351). Leave the `request(...)` default `timeout=1.0` (line 87) untouched so SessionStart keeps its one-second limit.
+4. `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/session_hook.py` — identical edit (FR-007).
+5. `plugins/graphhelm/README.md` — budget paragraph (lines 57–61) corrected (FR-006); the two honesty sentences preserved verbatim.
+
+**Not touched** (verified by `grep` that no other `"timeout"`/wait literal lives elsewhere in `plugins/`): `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/hooks.json` (Claude, stays 5/5, FR-005), both `plugin.json` manifests, `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, and every skill/eval document.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Introduce a host-keyed constant so Codex and Claude could use different waits — rejected; the spec's Assumptions state the shared script uses one wait bounded by the smaller (Codex) budget, and adding a keyed constant would be new surface the Keel card does not need.
+
+## R4. Test compatibility (why no test change is expected)
+
+- A grep of `session_hook.py` shows the only non-default timeout literal is line 351 (`timeout=3.0`); the default 1.0 (line 87) serves the SessionStart GET.
+- No test in `test_session_hook.py` asserts the literal `3.0` or `2.0`. The timing-bearing construct is `threading.Timer(1.25, …)` in `test_end_accepts_slow_matching_ack_without_extra_posts` — that is the ack-release instant, not an assertion on W. Every other `timeout=` in the test file (`post_release.wait(timeout=3)`, `post_started.wait(timeout=3)`, `communicate(timeout=6)`, subprocess `timeout=6`) is the *test's own* control timeout, larger than any hook wall time, so it is unaffected by lowering the hook wait from 3.0 to 2.0.
+- Conclusion: with W=2.0 (> 1.25), the suite is expected to pass **unmodified**, satisfying FR-008 without touching a test literal. (Had W been chosen ≤ 1.25, that one test would have needed updating in the same change — avoided by the value choice.)
+- Byte-identity is enforced by `test_codex_compatibility_package_keeps_one_hook_source`, which compares `read_bytes()` of both `session_hook.py` copies and both `codex-hooks.json` copies, plus the companion wiring (the `.codex-plugin/plugin.json["hooks"]` pointer and version equality). Applying byte-for-byte identical edits to each pair keeps it green.
+
+## R5. Which events/hosts clamp at which value
+
+**Decision** (the budget model; normative form in [`contracts/hook-budget-matrix.md`](contracts/hook-budget-matrix.md)):
+- Declared Codex SessionEnd = 3 = host clamp (no over-declaration). FR-001.
+- Declared Codex SessionStart = 5, unchanged (the observed clamp applies to SessionEnd only). FR-004.
+- Declared Claude SessionStart = 5, Claude SessionEnd = 5, unchanged. FR-005.
+- Shared end-ack wait W = 2.0 serves both hosts' SessionEnd (bounded by the smaller, Codex, budget); the SessionStart GET keeps its own 1.0 default.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: separate per-host waits — rejected (see R3).
+
+## R6. Single-source / byte-identity mechanism
+
+**Measured mechanism**: the companion package `graphhelm-codex-hooks` ships only `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, `hooks/codex-hooks.json`, and `hooks/session_hook.py` (no skills, no MCP server, no top-level `plugin.json`). At HEAD both `session_hook.py` copies hash identically and both `codex-hooks.json` copies hash identically (verified by SHA-256 of the two pairs: scripts `67A7B1CE…E895`, codex JSON `B671BBE6…B596`).
+
+**Decision**: the implementation must apply **byte-for-byte identical** edits to each pair (same bytes, same trailing newline), not merely semantically equal ones; after editing, re-hash both pairs and confirm equality (observer in `quickstart.md` S1).
+
+## R7. Open items
+
+None. Every provisional Technical Context item now has a decided, evidence-backed answer: value = 2.0 s; edit surface = 5 named files; observer = the existing in-process test module; language = Python 3.11; no new dependencies; English only. No NEEDS CLARIFICATION remains unresolved.
