+# Feature Specification: Codex SessionEnd Budget Clamp
+
+**Feature Branch**: N/A — unattended run, no `before_specify` branch hook registered; the feature directory `specs/001-bench` was created on the existing branch (`main`).
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft — validated against `checklists/requirements.md`, ready for `/speckit.clarify` or `/speckit.plan`.
+
+**Input**: User description: "Installed Codex clamps a `SessionEnd` hook command to three seconds, but both Codex hook files (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/codex-hooks.json` and `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/codex-hooks.json`) declare 5, and the end request in `session_hook.py` waits up to 3.0 seconds of socket inactivity, so Codex can kill the hook while it is still waiting. Declare 3 for Codex's SessionEnd and make the end acknowledgement wait fit inside it (both copies of `session_hook.py`). Claude's `hooks.json` keeps its 5-second budget. Correct the budget paragraph in `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`. The hook tests must stay green."
+
+## Context and problem
+
+When an agent session bound to a GraphHelm execution ends, the installed plugin sends one
+attributed session-end observation to the local Runtime and waits for a durable acknowledgement.
+The host agent imposes a hard time clamp on the SessionEnd hook command: **installed Codex
+clamps it to three seconds**. Today the plugin registers a larger budget than the host honors
+and waits almost as long as the host's clamp, so Codex can terminate the hook process while it
+is still waiting, and a durable acknowledgement that would have arrived is lost in flight.
+
+The same hook script and the same Codex hook registration exist as **byte-identical pairs** in
+two places (the main plugin package and the Codex compatibility companion package). Any change
+must keep those pairs identical, because the test suite verifies single-source consistency.
+Claude Code's own hook registration has a different, unchanged five-second budget.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Session-end acknowledgement survives the Codex time clamp (Priority: P1)
+
+An operator who bound a GraphHelm execution to a Codex session ends that session. They expect
+the session-end observation to either be durably acknowledged by the Runtime, or to remain
+retryable for the next chance — never silently killed by the host in the middle of its one
+acknowledgement wait.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the actual defect. Today, whenever the Runtime's durable
+acknowledgement arrives later than the shortened allowance but inside the host's clamp, the
+host can kill the hook mid-wait and the acknowledgement is lost in flight; the operator's
+end-of-session evidence is weaker than the plugin claims to deliver.
+
+**Independent Test**: With a bound session and a local stand-in Runtime, verify that (a) both
+Codex registrations declare the same three-second budget the installed host actually enforces,
+(b) an acknowledgement arriving inside the new wait is confirmed, and (c) silence beyond the
+new wait leaves the end unobserved and retryable under the same signal identity. None of these
+need Stories 2–3 to be true.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a Codex session bound to an execution, **when** it ends and the Runtime
+   acknowledges within the shortened acknowledgement wait, **then** delivery is confirmed,
+   repeated end invocations do not send the signal again, and no further request occurs.
+2. **Given** a Codex session bound to an execution, **when** the Runtime stays silent until
+   after the shortened acknowledgement wait expires, **then** the end is reported as
+   unobserved (never as success) and a later end retries with the identical signal body and
+   idempotency identity.
+3. **Given** both Codex hook registration files, **when** their SessionEnd command budget is
+   read, **then** each declares three seconds — equal to the installed Codex host clamp, with
+   no declaration exceeding what the host will honor.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The documented budgets match what the hosts actually do (Priority: P2)
+
+An operator reads the plugin guide to know how long a session-end acknowledgement may take
+before being left unobserved. They expect the documented per-host budgets to match the
+registered values and the installed hosts' real clamps, for both Codex and Claude Code.
+
+**Why this priority**: The guide currently states that both hosts bound the SessionEnd command
+process to five seconds, which contradicts the installed Codex clamp and is exactly the kind
+of stale number that caused this defect to go unnoticed. Wrong documentation misleads operators
+about when an end will be left unobserved, but does not by itself lose data.
+
+**Independent Test**: Compare the guide's budget paragraph against the three registration
+files and the installed-host clamp statement; every stated budget matches, and the
+honest-semantics sentences (a socket limit is not a wall-time deadline; a timeout remains
+unobserved and never becomes task success) are preserved.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the corrected plugin guide, **when** an operator compares its budget statements
+   to the registered values, **then** the Codex SessionEnd budget is stated as three seconds
+   (matching the installed host clamp), Claude's budget as five seconds, and the
+   acknowledgement wait is stated as fitting inside the Codex budget.
+2. **Given** the corrected plugin guide, **when** an operator looks for the existing
+   honesty statements, **then** the statements that the socket limit alone is not a wall-time
+   deadline and that a timeout remains unobserved are still present.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - One source of truth survives the change intact (Priority: P3)
+
+A maintainer upgrades either Codex package and expects both to behave identically, and expects
+the hook test suite to continue proving that the two packages have not drifted apart.
+
+**Why this priority**: The compatibility companion exists precisely because some Codex loaders
+skip the main package's hook registration. Drift between the two copies would mean different
+budgets depending on which package installed — silently reintroducing exactly this class of
+bug. It is a guardrail story, not a user-facing capability.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run the existing hook test suite and confirm it passes, including its
+byte-identity checks that the two script copies and the two Codex registration files match
+exactly, plus the compatibility-companion wiring checks.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the completed change, **when** the hook test suite runs, **then** every test
+   passes with no failures and no test weakened, deleted, or skipped to obtain the result.
+2. **Given** the completed change, **when** the two hook-script copies and the two Codex
+   registration files are compared byte for byte, **then** each pair is identical.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Acknowledgement slower than the shortened wait but faster than the old three-second wait**:
+  the end is now left unobserved rather than lost mid-flight inside the host clamp; the next
+  end invocation retries with the same signal body and idempotency identity, so no duplicate
+  or divergent signal is ever produced. This trade-off (accepting fewer very-slow
+  acknowledgements in exchange for never being killed while waiting) is the point of the fix
+  and is stated in the guide.
+- **Host terminates the process at the exact clamp boundary**: local state and locks stay
+  consistent; the OS releases the lock even when the host kills a timed-out hook; delivery is
+  simply retryable.
+- **Unbound session end**: still completely silent — no request, no output, no error — for
+  both hosts.
+- **Already-delivered session ends again**: still skipped without another request.
+- **Runtime refuses the signal (not silent, but non-acknowledging)**: still unobserved, not
+  recorded as delivered; secret material still never appears in the signal.
+- **Only one of the two Codex packages is installed or upgraded**: because the pairs remain
+  byte-identical, whichever package installs behaves identically to the other.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The Codex SessionEnd command hook MUST declare a three-second budget in **both**
+  Codex hook registration files (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/codex-hooks.json` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/codex-hooks.json`), matching the installed Codex host
+  clamp so no registration over-declares what the host will honor.
+- **FR-002**: The SessionEnd acknowledgement wait in the hook script MUST fit strictly inside
+  the three-second Codex budget — i.e., the maximum socket-inactivity wait for the end request
+  MUST be less than 3 seconds — in **both** copies of the hook script
+  (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/session_hook.py`), so the host cannot kill the hook
+  while it is still waiting for the acknowledgement.
+- **FR-003**: The shortened acknowledgement wait MUST retain the benefit of the earlier
+  extension: it MUST remain long enough to accept a durable acknowledgement slower than the
+  original one-second limit (i.e., strictly greater than 1 second).
+- **FR-004**: The Codex **SessionStart** registration MUST be left unchanged (it keeps its
+  current five-second declaration; the observed host clamp applies to SessionEnd only).
+- **FR-005**: Claude Code's hook registration (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/hooks.json`) MUST keep
+  its existing five-second budgets for both SessionStart and SessionEnd.
+- **FR-006**: The budget paragraph in `plugins/graphhelm/README.md` MUST be corrected so that
+  it states the true per-host picture: the end acknowledgement wait fits inside Codex's
+  three-second SessionEnd budget, Codex's SessionEnd is declared and clamped at three
+  seconds, and Claude keeps five seconds — while preserving the existing honesty statements
+  that the socket limit alone is not a wall-time deadline and that a timeout remains
+  unobserved and never becomes task success.
+- **FR-007**: After the change, the two hook-script copies MUST remain byte-identical, and the
+  two Codex hook registration files MUST remain byte-identical (the single-source invariant
+  that the compatibility companion test suite enforces).
+- **FR-008**: All existing hook tests MUST stay green after the change, with no test weakened,
+  deleted, or skipped to achieve the result. If any test literal encodes a value this change
+  corrects, that test is updated in the same change to assert the new contract.
+- **FR-009**: Delivery semantics MUST NOT change: the end signal keeps the same body and
+  idempotency identity across retries, a confirmed delivery skips later sends, a refusal or
+  timeout leaves the end unobserved rather than delivered, and unbound sessions remain silent.
+- **FR-010**: The change MUST be limited to the named budgets, the two hook-script copies, and
+  the documented paragraph — no other host, event, timeout, or behavioral setting is altered.
+
+### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*
+
+- **Session-end signal**: the attributed end-of-session observation the hook sends to the
+  Runtime. Key attributes: stable idempotency identity derived from host, execution, session,
+  and Runtime origin; actor identity scoped to the host session; explicit "not verified"
+  outcome wording. It never carries secret material.
+- **Local delivery record**: the per-session, per-execution, per-host, per-origin record of
+  whether the signal was durably acknowledged. Key attributes: delivered (yes/no), stored
+  signal copy, identity bindings. It is what makes repeated ends skip confirmed sends.
+- **Hook budget**: the time limit paired with a hook registration for one host and one event.
+  Two kinds exist and must not be conflated: the *declared* budget in the registration files,
+  and the *host-enforced clamp* the installed host actually applies (three seconds for Codex
+  SessionEnd, five seconds otherwise for these hooks).
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: Zero budget mismatches: in both Codex packages, the declared SessionEnd budget
+  equals the installed host's three-second clamp (2 of 2 registrations correct), while Claude's
+  registrations remain at five seconds (2 of 2 unchanged).
+- **SC-002**: Under local verification with a stand-in Runtime, an end acknowledgement arriving
+  at any point up to the shortened wait (e.g., 1.5 seconds) is confirmed as delivered; a
+  Runtime silent beyond the shortened wait leaves the end unobserved and retryable with the
+  same signal identity — in neither case does the host kill the hook while it is still inside
+  its acknowledgement wait.
+- **SC-003**: The plugin guide's budget statements contradict zero of the registered values:
+  every number in the corrected paragraph matches a registered value or a stated host clamp,
+  and the previously incorrect "both hosts … five seconds" claim no longer appears.
+- **SC-004**: The hook test suite completes with zero failures, zero weakened or removed
+  assertions, and the byte-identity checks between the two script copies and the two Codex
+  registration files still hold.
+- **SC-005**: The change stays bounded: only the Codex SessionEnd budget (both files), the end
+  acknowledgement wait (both scripts), and the documented budget paragraph change; no other
+  file, value, or behavior differs.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- The three-second clamp is imposed by the **installed** Codex host on SessionEnd hook commands
+  and is not adjustable from the plugin's side; declaring three therefore documents what the
+  host actually enforces instead of promising a budget the host would ignore.
+- The observed clamp applies to Codex **SessionEnd only**; the Codex SessionStart registration
+  keeps its current five-second declaration (FR-004).
+- The hook script is shared by both hosts, so one acknowledgement wait serves Claude's
+  five-second budget and Codex's three-second budget; Codex's smaller budget therefore bounds
+  the value. A concrete wait below three seconds and above one second is chosen during
+  planning so it leaves headroom for process start-up inside the host's wall-clock clamp while
+  still accepting acknowledgements slower than the original one-second limit.
+- The "socket inactivity" wait is not a wall-time guarantee (the code and guide already say
+  so); this feature keeps that framing and does not promise an absolute delivery deadline.
+- The existing hook tests assert behavior and cross-file byte identity rather than the exact
